<details class="methodology">
  <summary>Methodology</summary>
  <h4>1. Data</h4>
  <p>Official Elections Canada results by riding (2015, 2019, 2021 and 2025), the official transposition of
  the 2021 results onto the 2023 map, national polls since 2015 and their regional breakdowns, published by the polling firms, since
  August 2021 (Atlantic, Quebec, Ontario, Manitoba and Saskatchewan, Alberta, British Columbia). Riding
  boundaries of the 2023 Representation Order (Elections Canada), simplified for display.</p>
  <h4>2. Regional targets</h4>
  <p>For each region, the breakdowns published since the 2025 election are pooled with weight
  n·exp(−age/τ), age being measured on a clock where one campaign day counts as D days: τ = 120 days
  and D = 16 are those that minimise the seat error of the median scenario at the 2021 and 2025
  elections among the combinations tried, on the 327 broken-down polls. The targets are corrected for the mean regional bias of the eve polls of 2021 and
  2025, multiplied party by party by a shrinkage factor m²/(m² + s²/k), where m and s² are the mean and
  variance of the national gap between eve polls and results over the last k = 3 elections.</p>
  <h4>3. Ridings</h4>
  <p>Within each region, the 2025 results are carried to the target by transfer: a declining party loses
  the same proportion of its vote everywhere, modulated by (s/s̄)^(−β) with β = 0.5 (stronger retention
  where it is strong), and the released votes go to the gaining parties in proportion to their gains.
  Evaluated at known regional shares on the 2015→2019, 2019→2021 and 2021→2025 transitions, this
  transfer reduces the absolute seat error from 106 to 90 compared with a uniform proportional swing.</p>
  <h4>4. Uncertainty</h4>
  <p>Each draw combines a national shock and a regional shock in centred log-ratios (per-party standard
  deviations estimated on the errors of eve polls) and, for each riding, a local deviation drawn from the
  errors of the three transitions, centred by region. On the eve of the 2021 and 2025 elections, each
  evaluated with parameters estimated without it, all 12 per-party 90% seat intervals contain the
  result; the absolute seat error is 32 and 72 for the point
  projection, and 44 and 58 for the median scenario (medoid).</p>
  <h4>5. Government scenarios and certainty</h4>
  <p>Each draw is assigned to a single government scenario: majority when the first party reaches 172
  seats, minority otherwise. For a minority government, the parties whose seats, added to those of the
  first party, reach a majority are listed, excluding the Official Opposition (second party in seats): these parties hold the balance of power. In the certainty chart, each riding is assigned to its favourite party and classed by that party's probability of winning: safe (95% or more), likely (75 to 95%), lean (60 to 75%), toss-up (under 60%).</p>
</details>
